<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babri Masjid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1528">1528</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Babri Masjid was a mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in 1528–1529 under the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> believed it stood on the birthplace of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, and a long campaign by Hindu nationalist organisations culminated on 6 December 1992 in its demolition by a large crowd, followed by riots in which about 2,000 people died. In 2019 the Supreme Court awarded the site for a Hindu temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babri-masjid.jpg" alt="Babri Masjid" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Bourne[1][2] (1834–1912) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babri_Masjid_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
